:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe0b0813-21d9-52a2-8f85-afcbccc8b1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe0b0813-21d9-52a2-8f85-afcbccc8b1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306095} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3ddb3c-9f2c-53ce-a336-68b619092ee5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a605e37a-7d60-532f-958b-d76317ddbdf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181557" :question/problem "If $T={0,{-2,-3,-4},{9,10,11,12}}$, then $|T|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\n0,\\,{-2,-3,-4},\\,{9,10,11,12}\n$$\n\nNote that ${-2,-3,-4}$ and ${9,10,11,12}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1402c5-4acc-517b-957d-5e989e7f6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306098} {:answer/id #uuid "91361b35-1806-56a5-a47c-db4792c5aa9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91361b35-1806-56a5-a47c-db4792c5aa9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306099} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae666fef-4750-5c6e-be96-688bcbe5b75d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49131e72-c8a0-59f3-99af-aa1dff260098" :question/math-academy-id "q-181559" :question/problem "If $A={15,21,27,…,1\\,905},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n15,\\,21,\\,27,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6 \\\\  & =21-15 \\\\  & =27-21 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =15+(n-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =15+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6N+9 & =1\\,905 \\\\ 6N & =1\\,896 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,896}{6} \\\\ N & =316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 316}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $316$ elements, we must have $|A|=316.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e53f55-af8d-51fb-a296-b63e29304373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81e53f55-af8d-51fb-a296-b63e29304373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306103} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f183342-6bfa-5336-98cc-06e7ffe19937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e87e7b-6e84-5dd8-a2d8-4ac3f8e8903d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181563" :question/problem "If $A={-200,-193,-186,…,997},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-200,\\,-193,\\,-186,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =7 \\\\  & =-193-(-200) \\\\  & =-186-(-193) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-200+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-200+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7N-207 & =997 \\\\ 7N & =1\\,204 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,204}{7} \\\\ N & =172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $172$ elements, we must have $|A|=172.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34cd758-ad00-511f-b62f-2288203de635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34cd758-ad00-511f-b62f-2288203de635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2196f8-084b-5bf3-a44f-def1c362bf61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7c6f1d2-1982-573c-ba1d-3b71c29477d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181576" :question/problem "If $A={2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e023ebb9-f5d9-503a-a18e-f06bd6ea2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e023ebb9-f5d9-503a-a18e-f06bd6ea2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306124} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac30c343-541b-548d-bae1-0ef841ae60de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22256f74-ecc6-5ea7-b1e4-d18dff2b6d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-181582" :question/problem "If $A={1,6,36,216,…,2\\,176\\,782\\,336},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,6,\\,36,\\,216,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {6}^{n-1}={6}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{N-1} & =2\\,176\\,782\\,336 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{6}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N-1 & =12 \\\\ N & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 13}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $13$ elements, we must have $|A|=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3ff0e5d-b30f-5eb8-ba83-b400e6c1c76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ff0e5d-b30f-5eb8-ba83-b400e6c1c76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127} :answer-field/id #uuid "f290bfa4-b622-5f4d-8879-13eb7efca3a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b40818b3-dded-5ed0-bad5-7f30d36c8460" :question/math-academy-id "q-181583" :question/problem "If $A={1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-po